A call that can throw is an invoke, a terminator carrying a normal and an unwind
successor, and the scalar-call replacement tested for the Call opcode alone. At
-O0 with exceptions enabled that is most calls, so the mutator that stands for
"this call answered something else" never reached a throwing one: on the tree
this was written against it carried 462 mutants, none of them on a call that can
throw.

An invoke's value is live on its normal edge alone, so the replacement takes
over the uses first and the branch to that edge replaces the terminator after;
the unwind destination then loses this block from its predecessors, which is
what keeps a landing pad reached from elsewhere well formed. callbr stays out:
its successors are asm labels and branching past them is not a call replacement.

The sibling refusal in lib/IntrinsicReplacement.cpp is left alone: no Mull
mutator constructs it, so nothing it refuses is ever asked for.
--- a/lib/CallReplacement.cpp
+++ b/lib/CallReplacement.cpp
@@ -25,10 +25,15 @@
 
 bool CallReplacement::canMutate(llvm::Instruction *instruction) {
   assert(instruction);
-  if (instruction->getOpcode() != llvm::Instruction::Call) {
+  /// A call that can throw is an invoke, a terminator with a normal and an
+  /// unwind successor rather than a plain call.  Reaching only the Call opcode
+  /// left every throwing call unreplaced, which at -O0 with exceptions on is
+  /// most of them.  callbr stays out: its successors are asm labels.
+  if (instruction->getOpcode() != llvm::Instruction::Call &&
+      instruction->getOpcode() != llvm::Instruction::Invoke) {
     return false;
   }
-  auto *call = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::CallInst>(instruction);
+  auto *call = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::CallBase>(instruction);
   if (!call) {
     return false;
   }
@@ -53,9 +58,21 @@
 
 void CallReplacement::mutate(llvm::Instruction *instruction) {
   assert(instruction);
-  auto *call = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::CallInst>(instruction);
+  auto *call = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::CallBase>(instruction);
   auto returnType = call->getFunctionType()->getReturnType();
   auto replacement = constConstructor->constructValue(returnType);
+  /// An invoke is a terminator whose value is live on its normal edge alone,
+  /// so the replacement takes over the uses first and the branch to that edge
+  /// replaces the terminator after.  The unwind destination loses this block
+  /// from its predecessors, which is what keeps a landing pad reached from
+  /// elsewhere well formed.
+  if (auto *invoke = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::InvokeInst>(instruction)) {
+    invoke->replaceAllUsesWith(replacement);
+    llvm::BranchInst::Create(invoke->getNormalDest(), invoke->getIterator());
+    invoke->getUnwindDest()->removePredecessor(invoke->getParent());
+    invoke->eraseFromParent();
+    return;
+  }
   instruction->replaceAllUsesWith(replacement);
   instruction->eraseFromParent();
 }
